    "result_text": "The build is green. Here's what I changed and why.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe `customerCode` now takes the form `<REGION>-<HASH8>`, and every value derived from it follows automatically.\n\n**Two files touched:**\n\n1. **`CustomerCodes.java`** \u2014 rewrote `build` to produce `region(owner) + \"-\" + hash8(normalizedTelephone + lastName)`:\n   - `region(...)` derives the region from the postcode (2000\u2192NSW, 3000\u2192VIC, 4000\u2192QLD), falling back to the city, else `UNKNOWN`.\n   - `hash8(...)` computes SHA-256 and emits the first 8 upper-case hex characters (`%02X` over the first 4 bytes).\n   - Sequence numbers are gone.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 the `locality` mapping now delegates to `CustomerCodes.region(owner)`, so locality is derived from the same region that forms the identity rather than an independent switch.\n\n**What moved automatically (no edits needed):** `membershipNumber`, `checkDigit` (Luhn), and the create-audit record all already read `owner.getCustomerCode()`, so they now reflect the new identity \u2014 confirmed by the audit line `AUDIT - owner created ... customerCode=NSW-16E6609E`.\n\n## Keeping structural impact low\n\n- **Didn't touch the large `OwnerRestControllerV1`.** I kept `build(Owner, Collection<Owner>)`'s signature (the `existing` collection is now unused) so the single call site is unchanged \u2014 avoiding a change to a high-WMC class and a third touched file (the cost is multiplied by files touched).\n- **New behaviour lives in small, cohesive units.** `region` and `hash8` are new small methods in the tiny `CustomerCodes` class (low surrounding complexity), rather than growing an existing method.\n- **The `OwnerMapper` edit shrank a large inline switch to a one-line delegation**, keeping the changed unit simple and putting region logic in a single place.\n\n`region` replicates the old locality mapping exactly, so locality values are unchanged for every case (postcode, city fallback, unknown), and it's null-safe for seed owners with no `customerCode`. The new codes (\u226416 chars) fit the existing `customer_code VARCHAR(20)` column.",
